\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


An additive randomized encoding represents $f(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ through $\sum_i g_i(x_i)$ in a finite Abelian group. Correctness recovers $f$ from the sum; information-theoretic privacy requires a simulator using only the intended output, under the stated randomness convention.

In the LOCAL model vertices communicate synchronously along graph edges; messages and local computation are unrestricted and complexity is the number of rounds. After $t$ rounds a vertex can depend only on its radius-$t$ neighborhood and any supplied identifiers or randomness.

A code $C\subseteq\Sigma^N$ is a set of permitted length-$N$ words. Its Hamming minimum distance is the minimum number of unequal coordinates of two distinct words. For a linear $[N,K,D]_q$ code, rate is $K/N$ and relative distance is $D/N$; decoding recovers a codeword from corrupted observations.

A cryptographic security requirement compares an efficient adversary\textquotesingle s advantage with a negligible function of the security parameter. A negligible function is smaller than every inverse polynomial eventually; computational and information-theoretic security are different requirements.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Characterize the functions $f(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ that admit information-theoretically secure additive randomized encodings: local randomized maps $g_i(x_i)$ into a finite abelian group whose sum reveals $f$ and essentially nothing else about the inputs. \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2025/3}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
The meaning of essentially nothing else, shared versus private randomness, and exact versus approximate correctness are not specified.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record partially solved. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Characterize functions admitting perfectly or statistically private local additive encodings.

